1
00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:04.771
In this
lesson, you'll learn how to streamline

2
00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:09.209
the outputs from database operation
by incorporating a projection stage

3
00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:12.312
into the MongoDB aggregation pipeline.

4
00:00:12.479 --> 00:00:15.448
This will effectively reduce
the amount of data returned,

5
00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:18.418
optimizing performance and data handling.

6
00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:21.688
All right, let's get on with it.

7
00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:25.492
In the previous lesson,
you modified the fields within the documents

8
00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:30.463
returned after vector such and other stage
operations by using pydantic

9
00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:34.734
and specifying the attributes
that you wanted in the pydantic model.

10
00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:36.036
In this scenario,

11
00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:39.806
you're not using the entire fields
in the documents returned by results

12
00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:43.610
of the aggregation pipeline,
but you are leaving it to the application

13
00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.914
layer to handle the removal
of unwanted attributes or fields.

14
00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:53.053
This can have disadvantages
such as increased network traffic

15
00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:57.424
and processing time, as unwanted
data must still be transmitted

16
00:00:57.424 --> 00:01:00.693
and then filtered out at the application
layer.

17
00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:02.762
With a MongoDB database,

18
00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:07.033
the inclusion or exclusion of specific
fields can be handled

19
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.837
as another stage to add to the aggregation
pipeline.

20
00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:14.574
This is done through a technique
known as projection,

21
00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:19.746
which outputs the same number of documents
as the previous stage before it,

22
00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:24.584
but reduces
the fields return in each document.

23
00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:29.255
The projection technique
within MongoDB works by specifying fields

24
00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:32.959
to include or exclude
from the final documents.

25
00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:37.597
For example, the document
representation of the Mona Lisa painting

26
00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:42.597
we used in previous lesson, can be reduced
to a select few fields

27
00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:45.839
using the project operator in MongoDB,

28
00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:48.908
which you will get to implement
in the code section soon.

29
00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:54.347
There are several advantages to projection
with the inclusion of projection,

30
00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:58.551
the overall memory usage
at the application layer reduces

31
00:01:58.818 --> 00:02:03.556
as less data is passed as results
from database operations.

32
00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:06.726
This can also contribute
to reduce query execution time.

33
00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.529
And there is the case of security
and privacy.

34
00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:12.665
Take for example,
a finance application where personal

35
00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:15.869
information and sensitive
data are stored in documents.

36
00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:20.140
It can be useful to have the database
handle the logic of removing

37
00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.977
sensitive information
before being sent to downstream processes.

38
00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:27.180
This provides an overall improved sense

39
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.183
of security in the application.

